const cellParts = t("cell-theory-and-organelles", "Cell theory and organelle functions", "B", [
  s("Cells are units of life", "B2.1",
    "Cell theory states that living organisms consist of one or more cells, cells are the basic units of living structure and function, and new cells arise from existing cells. A cell is not simply an empty container around liquid. It has organized components and processes that meet needs. Viruses complicate discussions of living things but are not typical cells; this lesson's model concerns cellular organisms. Observations using microscopes helped establish the theory, while a diagram alone is not evidence that every depicted structure is visible.",
    "Compare adult-approved microscope images and labelled diagrams. Identify what can actually be seen and what is represented using other evidence; never collect or culture biological samples."),
  s("Components perform related functions", "B2.2",
    "The membrane controls exchanges with surroundings, cytoplasm supports many processes, and the nucleus contains genetic information guiding cell activities. Mitochondria help release usable energy from food through cellular respiration; they do not create energy from nothing. Vacuoles store substances. Chloroplasts carry out photosynthesis in suitable plant cells, and a cell wall provides support outside the membrane. Not every plant cell has chloroplasts, for example many root cells. A component's function depends on relationships with the rest of the cell.",
    "Label the original simplified plant-cell diagram and connect membrane, nucleus, chloroplast, and vacuole to functions. Note that component sizes and positions are illustrative."),
  s("Plant and animal cells share features", "B2.3",
    "Plant and animal cells share membranes, cytoplasm, nuclei in typical eukaryotic examples, and mitochondria. Plant cells commonly have a cellulose wall, a large central vacuole, and chloroplasts in photosynthetic tissues. Animal cells lack a cellulose wall and chloroplasts. Shapes vary within each group, so a rectangle and circle do not establish a universal rule. Compare labelled components and their functions rather than appearance alone. Specialized cells may depart from a textbook example; the simplified model is not every cell.",
    "Make a shared-and-different component chart using adult-approved diagrams. Include the root-cell chloroplast exception and one limitation of the textbook shapes."),
], [
  ["Which claim agrees with cell theory?", "New cells arise from existing cells", "Cells appear from non-living debris without prior cells", "Only large animals contain cells", "Cell theory connects structure and continuity of life. Organisms can be one cell or many, and cell division produces cells from existing cells."],
  ["Why is a cell more than an empty bag?", "Its organized components carry out interacting life processes", "It contains no structures", "All cells only store water", "Membranes, genetic information, and metabolic processes support functions. The bag analogy helps show a boundary but omits much of the organization."],
  ["What is the membrane's role?", "Regulate exchanges between cell and surroundings", "Produce every atom in the cell", "Replace all genetic information", "A selectively permeable membrane helps control movement. Regulation of exchange supports the cell's internal conditions without isolating it completely."],
  ["Must every plant cell contain chloroplasts?", "No; many non-photosynthetic cells lack them", "Yes; every root cell photosynthesizes", "No plant cell ever has them", "Chloroplasts support photosynthesis in appropriate tissues. A plant organism has specialized cells, so the green-leaf example should not be applied to every root or storage cell."],
], [["Cell theory", "An explanation of cells as units of life arising from cells"], ["Membrane", "A selective boundary regulating exchange"], ["Chloroplast", "A component supporting photosynthesis"]],
["Identify the diagram's outer support and selective boundary.", "Locate components using the labelled shapes.", "Connect each component to its supported function."]);
